  "limits": [
    "MPS has no float64 tensors, so the float64 signature cannot be served; the CPU kernel serves it",
    "the CPU kernel rejects float16/bfloat16 (\"sspmm\" not implemented) while this overlay serves them from the native SparseMPS mm; values are float16/bfloat16 arithmetic, the structure is identical",
    "bool raises on both backends",
    "invalid-input errors are RuntimeError like the CPU kernel but the shape messages are the CPU kernel's; dtype-mismatch and out-dtype messages are this overlay's",
    "a hybrid sparse self (sparse_dim other than 2) is rejected as Argument #1; the CPU kernel instead fails inside an internal size comparison",
    "the output nnz is data dependent, so the occupied-row count is read back to the host once per call; torch.unique_consecutive blocks for that count, which is the only synchronization, and its cost is a host round trip rather than a function of the operand size (see `sync`)",
    "that readback dominates small products, so the MPS call can be slower than the CPU kernel there; `caller` is such a case",
    "the product is computed by the native sparse mm, which allocates a dense m x n intermediate before the occupied rows are gathered; memory is therefore O(m*n) in addition to the COO result",
    "float64/complex128 feed neither MPS tensors nor this overlay; complex64 is served"
  ],
